🔤 Day 16: Palindrome String Checker


📌 Project Overview

This project is a Palindrome String Checker built using Python.
The program asks the user to enter a word or phrase and checks whether it reads the same forwards and backwards.


For example:

madam → madam

So:

madam is a palindrome.

The program is also case-insensitive, meaning:

Madam
MADAM
madam

are all treated the same.

This project introduces an important new Python concept:



🔄 String slicing with [::-1] to reverse a string.

✨ Features


🔤 1. Check a Word or Phrase

The program accepts a word or phrase:

wrd0 = input("Enter a word or (press q to Quite): ")

Example:

Enter a word or (press q to Quite): madam


🔡 2. Case-Insensitive Checking

The program converts the input to lowercase:

wrd1 = wrd0.lower()

For example:

Madam

becomes:

madam

This means uppercase and lowercase letters don't affect the palindrome check.


🚫 3. Ignore Spaces

You implemented the first bonus:

wrd = wrd1.replace(" ", "")

For example:

nose son

becomes:

noseson

Then the program checks:

noseson

against its reverse:

noseson

So it is detected as a palindrome.


🔄 4. Reverse the String

The main new concept is:

r_wrd = wrd[::-1]

For example:

text = "hello"

After:

text[::-1]

the result is:

olleh


🔁 5. Multiple Checks

You also implemented the second bonus.

The program uses:

while True:

so the user can check multiple words or phrases without restarting the program.

The program continues until the user enters:

q


🚪 6. Quit Option

If the user enters q, the program exits:

if wrd == "q":
    ...
    break


⚠️ 7. Empty Input Validation

You also added a useful validation:

if wrd == "":
    print("Please enter an actual word or phrase")

So pressing Enter without entering anything produces a message instead of performing the palindrome check.


